#!/../bin/madc
// Range-for over a KEY-subscripted container is a compile ERROR, not a crash.
//
// std::map has both size() and operator[], but `m[i]` is a keyed
// lookup-that-inserts — never the i'th element. The real range protocol is
// begin()/end(), and it yields std::pair<const K, V>, so BOTH canon compilers
// reject this exact source:
//   g++ 13.3   error: cannot convert 'std::pair<const int, int>' to 'int' in
//              initialization
//   clang 18   error: no viable conversion from 'std::pair<const int, int>' to
//              'int'
// madc used to trust the two METHOD NAMES, emit the index loop anyway, and hand
// the loop counter to `map::operator[](const key_type &)` — which wants a
// pointer. That SIGSEGV'd at (nil). The protocol match is type-checked now, and
// a keyed container (one that names a key_type) has no index protocol at all.
//
// This expectation is PERMANENT: begin()/end() iteration HAS landed now
// (tests/testforeachiter.mad), and a map element is still a pair that cannot
// initialize an `int`, exactly as canon says.
//
// WHAT CHANGED WITH THE ITERATOR PROTOCOL: madc no longer refuses the CONTAINER
// — it iterates it — so the rejection has moved to the element ASSIGNMENT, where
// c2mir reports it. The `.expect_err` fixture tracks that.
//
// It is deliberately c2mir's diagnostic and not a front-end one. A front-end
// message naming both types the way g++ does would need a real convertibility
// predicate, and madc has none: the direction is NOT simply "an aggregate cannot
// initialize a scalar", because a class with `operator int()` legitimately can,
// and a `const char *` element legitimately initializes a `std::string` loop
// variable. Guessing at that lattice to improve one message would reject working
// code. The rejection itself is correct, loud, and exits nonzero, which is what
// this test gates.
#include <map>
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
	std::map<int,int> m;
	m[1] = 10;
	m[2] = 20;
	for (int v : m)
		std::cout << v << std::endl;
	return 0;
}
